## Verdict
<A short, honest, human-toned note on where this is headed:
- **money** — marketable, a real gap, worth pursuing commercially.
- **fame** — technically strong or interesting, good portfolio/CV
  material, but not a business.
- **private** — genuinely gives an edge (an advantage in trading,
  scraping, a job search, a game, a market — something competitive), and
  the edge depends on staying non-public. This is a confident, deliberate
  call to keep it back, not a hedge — different from "neither," which is
  "can't tell yet." Say what the edge is and why sharing it would blunt it.
- **expand** — has a clear direction and real potential, but needs more
  built before money or fame can be honestly judged. More hopeful than
  "neither" — there's a path, it's just not walked yet.
- **merge** — more valuable combined with another project in this same
  portfolio than continued alone (name it in `merge_with` and explain
  why — e.g. two half-built tools solving adjacent problems, or one
  project's data feeding another's use case).
- **delete** — an active recommendation to remove it, not just neglect
  it. Use this when the technology is genuinely obsolete with no path
  forward, when it was a pure tutorial/learning exercise with no lasting
  value beyond having been practice, or when a repo is simply the wrong
  medium for what it holds (e.g. a data dump or personal archive that
  belongs in a proper store, not version control). Different from
  "neither": neither is "can't tell yet, still open"; delete is a closed
  case with an actual verdict.
- **neither** — thin, unclear, or stalled without a visible path forward.
  The honest "I can't yet tell, and there's no obvious next step" bucket.

Say why in one or two sentences — this is the part a human skimming the
portfolio actually reads. Don't hedge into "could be either"; pick the one
that's true right now, given what's actually on disk.

If a project genuinely doesn't fit any of these — not a bad-fit
approximation, but a real gap in the categories above — pick the closest
one anyway (so the row still sorts sensibly) and add a line starting
"**Suggested new category:**" in the Verdict section explaining what's
missing and why. The taxonomy above grew out of exactly this kind of
feedback; don't force a bad fit silently.>

